\section{Part 1: Solving Differential Equations and Systems of Linear Algebraic Equations}

One of the key skills in Mathematics and Engineering is solving differential equations and systems of linear algebraic equations. Let's take a look at some examples using Latex.

\subsection{Example 1: Solving a Simple ODE}

Consider the following first-order ordinary differential equation:

$$ y'(t) = ay(t) + b, \quad y(0) = y_0 $$

where $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $y(t)$ is the function we are trying to find.

We can solve this equation analytically using LaTeX with the package `odeint`. Here's an example code:

\begin{lstlisting}
\usepackage{odeint}

\newcommand{\exampleODE}{%
  \begin{equation}
    y'(t) = a y(t) + b
  \end{equation}

  % Define initial conditions and parameters
  \begin{lstlisting}
    y0 = 0;
    b = 1;
    a = 0.5;
    tspan = [0, 10];
  \end{lstlisting}

  % Solve the equation with ode45 (Runge-Kutta 4-5) solver
  \begin{lstlisting}
    [t, y] = ode45(@(t,y) a*y + b, tspan, y0);
  \end{lstlisting}

  % Plot the solution
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[]
      \addplot[thin, blue] {y};
      \node[right] at (current axis.abscissa, current axis.y1) {$y(t)$};
    \end{axis}
  \end{tikzpicture}
}

\exampleODE
\end{lstlisting}

